    },
    "body": "Affects `RESTMusicService.<init>` in `app/src/main/java/github/paroj/dsub2000/service/RESTMusicService.java`.\n\nI found that `RESTMusicService` built an anonymous trust manager with empty certificate checks and passed it to `SSLContext.init`. It also installed a hostname verifier that accepted every hostname, which does not meet the `SSLContextSpec` usage rule requiring trust managers from a `TrustManagerFactory`.\n\nI replaced those components with trust managers initialized from the platform default trust store and the default hostname verifier. The existing HTTPS configuration path and public getters remain, but now return platform-validating components. If TLS-context initialization fails, the service retains the platform default socket factory and logs a warning.\n\nI found this while running the app under RVSec, a runtime checker for how apps use the Java crypto and TLS APIs.\n\nThe project builds with this change (`:app:assembleFlossDebug`).\n\nOne thing to decide: with this change the \"allow insecure connections\" setting no longer skips certificate checks, so a server with a self-signed certificate will only connect if its certificate is installed on the device. If you'd rather keep that option working, I can limit the change to the default path. This follows up on #177.\n\nI'm a Computer Engineering student at the University of Brasília (UnB), working on a research project on Android app analysis. Happy to adjust this to the project's conventions.",
